$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{7x^2}{x-x^3}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[7x^2\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[x-x^3]}\qquad\gray{\text{l’Hôpital’s rule}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{14x}{1-3x^2}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[14x\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[1-3x^2]}\qquad\gray{\text{l’Hôpital’s rule}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{14}{-6x}
\\\\
&=0
\end{align}$

Note that we used l’Hôpital’s rule *twice*, because the first time we used it, we ended with the indeterminate form $\dfrac{\infty}{-\infty}$ too.

Also note that we were only able to use l’Hôpital’s rule because the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[14x\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[1-3x^2]}$ can actually be determined.
